WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.570
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.040
Oh, Angela, we have a good episode today.

9
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.280
It is so good.

10
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:38.000
It's so simple, so wonderful, so much tension, conflict, a little bit of drama.

11
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.080
It's all there and some laughs.

12
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.240
It is called Did I Stutter?

13
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:51.980
It is season four, episode 16, written by Brent Forrester and Justin Spitzer and directed by Randall Einhorn.

14
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.560
We've got a lot to cover.

15
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.520
I say get into that summary, lady.

16
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:56.560
Here it is.

17
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:03.000
Stanley challenges Michael's authority during a conference room meeting, leaving Michael speechless.

18
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:03.920
Mm-hmm.

19
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:12.100
Later, Michael responds to Stanley's insubordination by attempting to scare him into an attitude adjustment with a fake firing.

20
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.320
What is with Michael and the fake firings?

21
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.320
I don't know.

22
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.940
He thinks it's like a go-to.

23
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.180
I mean, it did not go well with Pam.

24
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.180
No, he should have abandoned this whole idea back then.

25
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:24.600
Yeah.

26
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.320
But he's still doing it.

27
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:33.300
Meanwhile, Dwight decides to buy Andy's car at a discount only to immediately flip it for more money.

28
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:33.960
Mm-hmm.

29
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.080
So shrewd.

30
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.300
Did he shrewd it?

31
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.980
He shrewded it.

32
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:47.880
Pam has to deal with the unexpected inconvenience of wearing her backup glasses to work after spending the night at a friend's house.

33
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.900
Her backup glasses from junior high?

34
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:51.940
Perhaps.

35
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.100
I'll have a lot to share about that.

36
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:56.840
I bet.

37
00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:57.280
I bet.

38
00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.160
All right.

39
00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.620
Fast fact number one.

40
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.580
We're going to talk a little bit about the inspiration for this episode.

41
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.260
We had a fan question from Bianca O and Katie W.

42
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.120
They wanted to know who came up with the idea for Stanley to sass Michael.

43
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:13.800
It was me.

44
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:14.800
No, I'm kidding.

45
00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.060
Well, Angela, you do love a sass.

46
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.500
I love the sass.

47
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.100
Well, we traded messages with writer Brent Forrester about this episode,

48
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:34.820
and he told us he remembered being very excited when Greg assigned them the script because it was the biggest Stanley story he had ever done at that point.

49
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:40.640
It was really the first time that Stanley had ever been the sole antagonist in a Michael story.

50
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.380
I remember we were all so excited for Leslie.

51
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:51.840
Just as actors, to be able to have that big moment with Michael and to go head-to-head with Steve, that was like, you hoped for that, right?

52
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.060
Oh, yeah.

53
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.140
Yeah, this was huge.

54
00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:00.580
Brent remembers Greg told him with great delight what the game of this episode was.

55
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.300
It should be this.

56
00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:09.820
Michael is an overpowering boss to everyone in the office but is totally unable to stand up to one person, Stanley.

57
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:17.440
No one ever dares to challenge Michael this blatantly, so when Stanley snaps at him, Michael just has no playbook for dealing with it.

58
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:26.820
But he said that Mindy referred to this as reversal of status comedy, and it was the first time Brent had ever heard that term.

59
00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.920
Mindy, you're so smart.

60
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.540
I know, right?

61
00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.120
I remember doing Commedia dell'arte in theater school, Angela.

62
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:36.560
No, here we go.

63
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.620
Did you ever do Commedia?

64
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:38.780
No, Jenna.

65
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.640
I did my improv and my stand-up and my sketch comedy, so just let's hear it.

66
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:51.520
Well, it's really very interesting because Commedia dates back to long, long ago, and it is really all about status.

67
00:03:52.060 --> 00:04:01.480
And you're constantly switching status between these archetypal characters, and this has some real Commedia elements for all you theater nerds out there,

68
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.520
this reversal of status comedy.

69
00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.000
No, it's fantastic.

70
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.440
I mean, even in improv, we would make someone, when you walk on stage, choose status, high or low.

71
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:18.620
And if you constantly played high status, because some people, it's their go-to in comedy, our teacher would make you reverse it and play low status.

72
00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.340
It was always a really cool exercise.

73
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:21.900
Okay, getting a little nerdy.

74
00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.400
Okay, we can move on.

75
00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.500
No, I love that we're nerding out on status comedy.

76
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.620
Guys, watch comedy shows and look for status and reversal of status.

77
00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:31.600
That's your homework.

78
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.140
We're making your television viewing homework now.

79
00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:35.720
You're welcome.

80
00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:36.920
We're giving you homework.

81
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:48.240
Fast fact number two, Brent told us that this episode produced a mantra for the writer's room and a standard for which all episodes would now be held.

82
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:49.300
What?

83
00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:56.140
Here were the three words that applied to this episode that they believed make a great office episode.

84
00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.380
Small, real, relatable.

85
00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.200
Brent said by normal TV standards, almost nothing happens in this episode.

86
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.800
Stan Lee insults Michael.

87
00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.660
Michael doesn't know how to respond.

88
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.940
He said on any other show, this would have been considered a totally insufficient plot for 30 minutes of TV.

89
00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:24.440
But he said at this point in the series, they were so confident in the characters and their dynamics that they fully committed to this very subtle interpersonal story.

90
00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:30.260
And they wrote this slogan in the writer's room, small, real, relatable.

91
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.760
And he said that he believes that this is the signature of the office at its beSt.

92
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:36.860
I agree.

93
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.140
I always love those types of episodes.

94
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:44.180
I mean, I know it comes before this one, but I felt like health care was in that vein, you know?

95
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:44.820
Yes.

96
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.160
But yeah, I love small, real, relatable.

97
00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:48.880
I love that kind of comedy.

98
00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:49.440
Mm-hmm.

99
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.480
All right, fast fact number three, Angela, Pam's glasses.

100
00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:54.700
Oh, yeah.

101
00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.220
We got a lot of mail.

102
00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:57.300
Abby S.

103
00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:58.060
and Amy G.

104
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.620
want to know, Jenna, is your eyesight as bad as Pam's?

105
00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.360
And then Kat F., Jessica M., and Rachel G.

106
00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:09.920
want to know how many pairs of glasses were given as options for this episode?

107
00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:15.040
I remember when we were filming this, at that time, you didn't need glasses.

108
00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.220
You had great eyesight.

109
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:17.960
Yes, that's right.

110
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.840
I did not wear glasses.

111
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.060
I do now, but I didn't then.

112
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.420
Those are totally fake glasses.

113
00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:30.240
Phil Shea came on over with his tray of frames.

114
00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:37.160
Greg was there, and we went through, and I had to put them on, and we just talked about which ones did we like.

115
00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:44.000
We did discuss, Angela, that these were frames that dated back to maybe, like, years for Pam.

116
00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.180
Like you said, are these her junior high glasses?

117
00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:50.100
Well, you said something interesting on the DVD commentary for this episode.

118
00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:56.920
You said you gave yourself your own little actor backstory, right, that didn't exist on the page, hadn't been discussed with the writers.

119
00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:04.080
And for you, these glasses were the pair that Pam leaves in the glove box of her car for just in case.

120
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:04.900
Yes.

121
00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.460
Here's the crazy thing about these glasses.

122
00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:14.400
Greg really, really wanted for my eyes to look super giant when I put on the glasses.

123
00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:22.180
But in order for that to be true, they would have had to put a true prescription in the lenses to magnify my eyes.

124
00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.240
And I said, but then I wouldn't be able to see all day.

125
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.000
Right.

126
00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:28.220
But listen to what Greg said.

127
00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.560
Greg said, well, here's what we'll do.

128
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:37.540
We'll give you contact lenses to degrade your vision and then correct your vision with the glasses.

129
00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.520
Greg, what?

130
00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:48.680
And I said, that sounds like a lot of effort for these few scenes where Pam wears her glasses.

131
00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.840
I've never worn contacts before.

132
00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.160
This sounds like it has the potential to be a real disaster.

133
00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.900
And possibly damaging to my future eyesight.

134
00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:02.600
So I told this to Lee as we were watching the episode.

135
00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:06.760
I said, can you believe that Greg wanted me to wear like contact lenses and then glasses?

136
00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.420
And Lee said, oh, that would have been funny.

137
00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:10.180
Greg was right.

138
00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.060
You should have done it.

139
00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:17.980
Oh, and I was like, oh, Greg will be so happy to hear that because everybody pooh-poohed this idea.

140
00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.500
Like props, hair and makeup, production.

141
00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:26.640
Everybody was like, Greg, the potential for mishap with this idea is too great.

142
00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:27.800
We can't have it.

143
00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.840
So Greg was very reluctant to let it go.

144
00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:31.460
But he did.

145
00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.220
He was like, all right, fine, fine.

146
00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.000
So I think Greg would be really happy to know that my husband backs up his idea.

147
00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:45.500
Well, I have a few more tidbits about Pam's glasses, but I will sprinkle them into the episode as we say.

148
00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.000
And that finalizes my fast facts, lady.

149
00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:49.800
Well, I have two things to share.

150
00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.080
First of all, little tidbit from the DVD commentary.

151
00:08:53.520 --> 00:09:01.140
Randall Einhorn, who directed this episode, said that the editor's assembly cut came in at 41 minutes.

152
00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:08.200
And then he took that 41 minutes and he got it down to 31 minutes for his director's cut.

153
00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:08.840
31.

154
00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.340
He didn't want to lose any more.

155
00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.320
And they had to get it down to 22 minutes to air it on NBC.

156
00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.100
So there are a ton of deleted scenes.

157
00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:25.500
But in addition to that, there's all these other scenes that they didn't even get to put in the deleted scenes.

158
00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:26.340
There's more.

159
00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:27.240
Wow.

160
00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:28.080
Yes.

161
00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:31.780
And Jenna, here's my big thing I wanted to share with you.

162
00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:38.600
As I was prepping for the podcast, I found my shooting draft of Did I Stutter?

163
00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:39.320
What?

164
00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.000
I found the script.

165
00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:43.560
With all of those lost scenes?

166
00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:44.500
Yes.

167
00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.680
And some we actually shot.

168
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.800
They're not even in the deleted scenes.

169
00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:49.880
They're out there somewhere.

170
00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:57.600
But I found my shooting draft and there are some really hilarious discrepancies that I am going to share with you.

171
00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:00.940
There is a Jim Pam moment that is going to make your mouth drop.

172
00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.460
But I am going to sprinkle these in.

173
00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:04.380
I'm so excited.

174
00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:05.840
I had my shooting draft.

175
00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.220
I am so jealous.

176
00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:10.700
I couldn't believe it.

177
00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:11.820
I can't wait.

178
00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:19.160
So now with that knowledge, I want you to know that we filmed this episode starting March 31st of 2008.

179
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.380
We got an email from Kent Sabornak that said,

180
00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.880
Hey, you guys, just so you know, while we're filming Did I Stutter,

181
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.120
Canadian Entertainment Tonight is coming by the set to do interviews in the warehouse,

182
00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:34.440
and they're going to get B-roll in the main bullpen.

183
00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:36.500
So we had Canadian E.T.

184
00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.720
here the week of Did I Stutter?

185
00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:39.660
Amazing.

186
00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:43.480
I love how you save every freaking email, Angela.

187
00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:47.920
It is so cool and really paying off.

188
00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.380
I'm just a pack rat.

189
00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.600
I'm a digital pack rat and a real life pack rat.

190
00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.580
Well, let's take a break because when we come back, we've got a lot of stuff.

191
00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:08.300
All right.

192
00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.200
Well, we are back.

193
00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.040
Michael bursts into the bullpen with all of this excitement because you guys,

194
00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.660
there is wet cement in the parking lot, and this is a lifelong dream of his.

195
00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.000
He's got to do something, write something.

196
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:22.020
What is he going to do?

197
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:30.000
And Jenna, when I rewatched this, I started laughing because I remembered how hard Phyllis kept breaking.

198
00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:33.380
How hard we all kept breaking at Phyllis.

199
00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:33.900
Breaking.

200
00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:39.500
Yes, because she has an idea for Michael of what he could write in the cement.

201
00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.140
Oh, but she can't remember it.

202
00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.100
What was it again?

203
00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:44.820
And Michael's going, come on, come on, come on.

204
00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:45.320
Go, go.

205
00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:45.760
It's drying.

206
00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:53.220
And watching Phyllis trying not to laugh, like fighting it so hard, we were all cracking up.

207
00:11:53.380 --> 00:12:00.800
And if you go to 37 seconds, you can see Rain and John smiling really big because we had all just been laughing.

208
00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.980
Well, I just think it's very funny that last week Michael got gum in his hair because he found something in the parking lot.

209
00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.780
And now, I mean, the cement.

210
00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:15.200
The parking lot has been a real source of excitement for him lately.

211
00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:16.340
And trouble.

212
00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:19.780
I kind of just want to say, Michael, get out of your car and go straight inside.

213
00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:20.760
Exactly.

214
00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:27.200
Somehow, Kelly suggests via Pam that Michael put his handprints.

215
00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:28.740
Pam translates Kelly.

216
00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:29.640
Yes.

217
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.440
Pam speaks Kelly for Michael.

218
00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:35.760
But Jim one-ups it and says, you should put your face in the cement.

219
00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.080
Oh, ding, ding, ding.

220
00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:39.400
Michael is so excited.

221
00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:40.580
This is what's going to happen.

222
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.880
And so much went into this one gag.

223
00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.000
Oh, yes.

224
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:49.420
This simple sentence in the script was like a whole production.

225
00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:56.220
We got some fan questions from Anna V, Abby M, Sonia C, and Gabby C.

226
00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:00.960
Please give us all the details about this hilarious cold open.

227
00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.200
Did Steve really put his face in cement?

228
00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:07.180
How many times did he have to do it?

229
00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:10.340
Well, it was not actual cement.

230
00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:18.100
Kent Sabornak told me that they had an outside prop house make an organic mixture to look like cement.

231
00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:25.460
It was made out of water and some ground oatmeal and a few other ingredients to create a wet cement look.

232
00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:29.640
Jenna, I remember touching it and it kind of felt like a putty, squishy.

233
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:30.220
Yeah.

234
00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:34.620
Kent said that we put that Vaseline all over Steve's face,

235
00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.860
partially so that we would have quick cleanup and we could do multiple takes.

236
00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:41.000
And guess what?

237
00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:42.180
It wasn't Vaseline.

238
00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:46.600
The container he's holding says Vaseline, but Vaseline is too thick.

239
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.640
So it was actually KY jelly.

240
00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.020
I remember that.

241
00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:52.940
Yes.

242
00:13:53.300 --> 00:14:00.200
And then they saran wrapped Steve's head and put this like really big rubber band around it and then stuck a straw in there.

243
