349
00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.580
Let me tell you the history of Mad Libs.

350
00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:08.980
Okay.

351
00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:16.480
Mad Libs was invented in 1953 by two men, Leonard Stern and Roger Price.

352
00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:20.440
Roger Price had worked as a writer on The Bob Hope Show.

353
00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:24.340
Leonard Stern was his friend who worked as a writer on The Honeymooners.

354
00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:25.900
They were comedy writers.

355
00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:33.320
One day, Roger went to visit Leonard at work and Leonard was working on a script for The

356
00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:33.840
Honeymooners.

357
00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:40.060
He was trying to figure out the right word to describe the nose of Ralph Cramden's new

358
00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:40.500
boss.

359
00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:46.680
Before he could explain the whole thing, he just said to his friend, I need an adjective.

360
00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:51.260
And Price jumped in and started throwing out words like clumsy, naked.

361
00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:53.020
He just started throwing out adjectives.

362
00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:55.980
So they thought this was so funny, right?

363
00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:58.880
Like, oh, a clumsy nose, a naked nose.

364
00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:03.420
So this gave them the idea to write a bunch of stories with missing words.

365
00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:06.460
They took them to a cocktail party with their friends.

366
00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:09.520
They had people fill in the blanks without knowing the context.

367
00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.860
And it was a huge hit.

368
00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:13.580
People thought this was a hoot.

369
00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:17.320
They tried to get these stories published, but publishers passed.

370
00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:18.220
Nobody wanted them.

371
00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:19.780
They said, we don't get it.

372
00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:22.200
Maybe sell it to a game company or something.

373
00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:23.520
It sounds like a party game.

374
00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:29.780
So they sat on the idea for five years and then they decided to self-publish.

375
00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.460
But now they needed a name for it, right?

376
00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:32.760
Yeah.

377
00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:33.860
They had all their stories.

378
00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:34.980
They're self-publishing.

379
00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.100
Get this.

380
00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:42.080
These two guys, they're out having breakfast in New York and they overheard an agent arguing

381
00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.060
with his client at another table.

382
00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:49.200
And the agent was trying to tell this actor that he had to recite a certain script for

383
00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:50.180
an upcoming interview.

384
00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:54.100
And the actor was saying, no, I want to ad-lib my interview.

385
00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:55.360
I want to ad-lib it.

386
00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:59.420
And the agent said, that would be mad to ad-lib an interview.

387
00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.340
And that's where the name Mad Lib came from.

388
00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:08.420
And now they have sold over 110 million copies of Mad Lib's books.

389
00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:10.080
That was amazing.

390
00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.580
I loved every single moment of that.

391
00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:19.340
I also find it really inspiring that you can have this once-in-a-lifetime idea and, you

392
00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:24.680
know, don't let go of it, even if it takes you six years to see it come, like, to fruition.

393
00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:25.640
Yeah.

394
00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:28.760
They were on to something and they knew it and they made it happen.

395
00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:29.740
I love that.

396
00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:30.660
